= Grade by grade

Each grade band from VB to V8+ gets a guide in the same six parts, from what problems look like to why people stall. Read the guide for your grade and the one above it. If you climb most V4s, read the V4 guide, the step to V5 (@grades-v4-v5) and the V5 guide.

*Indoor grades are local.* #ix("Grades!indoor variation") A #jt("setter") (the person who designs a problem) grades it against the other problems in the same gym, so the same grade can feel different elsewhere #src("F27"). Many walls grade by #jt("circuit") (a colour that spans two or three grades) #src("F26"). #ix("Circuits") Treat the guides as a picture of what is typical, not as a test. Chapter 2 explains how gym grades vary. The appendix converts V-grades to #jt("Font grade", body: [Font grades]) (the European scale).

*The badges.* Each band lists every technique whose Part II badge, such as #badge(4), falls at that grade. The badges are judgement. No coaching scheme, such as the UK's NICAS, ties named techniques to V-grades #src("F28", "F32"). The nearest thing is the technique clinics gyms run for named grade bands, and this chapter leans on those #src("F33", "F34", "F35").

*The physical side.* The max-hang numbers are averages across men and women, from a large database of tested climbers #src("F1"). Climbers at the same grade differ widely. The descriptions under "What problems look like" are judgement, not measurement.

#notebox[How long climbers have been climbing][
#ix("Progression!years climbing")
Lattice Training#ix("Lattice Training") holds data on about 65,000 climbers. Climbers at V1–V4 report 5.8 years of climbing on average. The average rises to 8.6 years at V5–V7 and 9.7 years at V8–V11 #src("F2"). Lattice's author suspects that casual long-time climbers inflate the V1–V4 figure, and calls that a hunch without data.

Older climbers at a grade report more years of climbing.#ix("Age!years climbing") At V8, climbers under 25 report 3.8 years on average. Climbers over 40 report 13.0 years #src("F2").

Haven't climbed a V5 after a year? These figures give you no reason to think that's slow. All of them are self-reported. Lattice, a training company, reports how long climbers at a grade have been climbing, not how long the grade took. One forum thread does ask what grade people reached after a year. Its answers range from about V4 outdoors to V8 indoors, but the posters are self-selected #src("F47").
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ VB–V0

== VB–V0 (Font~3–\u{2060}4+) <grade-0>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at VB–V0][
  + Watch each foot onto its hold before you weight it (#tref("precise-footwork", body: [precise footwork], show-page: false)).
  + Pause on #tref("straight-arms", body: [straight arms], show-page: false) on an overhang and stand tall on a vertical wall.
  + Stand up on your legs; let your arms only steer.
  + Say which hand goes to each hold before you start.
  + Jump off from low down and land with bent knees.
]

==== What problems look like

The wall is vertical, a gentle #tref("slab", body: [slab]) (leaning back) or a slight #tref("overhang", body: [overhang]). The holds are #tref("jug", body: [jugs]) and deep edges you can wrap your fingers over. Each foothold takes your whole forefoot. Problems have four to eight moves, laid out like a ladder with a foothold under each reach. No move needs momentum, so you can stop and hang anywhere.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Three habits carry you through this band and every band after it. Place each foot exactly (#tref("precise-footwork", inner: true)). When you pause on an overhang, hang on #tref("straight-arms", body: [straight arms]) with your shoulders set; on a vertical wall, stand tall. Move up by standing on your feet, not by pulling with your arms. Learn to jump off and land too#ix("Falling!practice") (@cons-falling): NICAS and the DAV#ix("DAV") both start their schemes with how to boulder safely #src("F28", "F32").

#grade-techniques(0)

==== Focus

- Look at each foothold until your shoe is on it. Then put your weight on it. Place the inside edge of your big toe on the best part of the hold.
- Climb many different problems each session. One Lattice post advises 6–12 months of climbing before fingerboard training#ix("Fingerboard!when to start") #src("F6"). Coaches differ on gentle hangs (@cons-adult).
- Learn how your gym marks problems: which holds are the start, how to finish and which colour is which grade #src("F26").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Find the start holds (often one marked hold per hand) and the finish hold.
- Count the hand moves. Say which hand goes to each hold.
- Point out the foothold for each reach. If a foothold sits right under your next handhold, step up onto it before you reach.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific. Any healthy adult has the strength for this band.

==== Why people stall here

- *Bent arms.* You get #jt("pump", body: [pumped]) (tired, swollen forearms) after a few moves, and coaches put it down to bent elbows. Fix (coaching advice): pause before every reach. Straighten your arms on an overhang and stand tall on a vertical wall.
- *Noisy feet.* Your shoes scuff and slip off. Fix: climb whole problems placing every foot without a sound.
- *Fear of falling.* Fix: practise falling from low down, a little higher each session (@cons-falling).

One blog says most people get through this band in their first few sessions #src("F48").

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V1

== V1 (Font 5) <grade-1>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V1][
  + Shift your hips over the pushing foot before every reach.
  + Stand on the inside #tref("edging", body: [edge], show-page: false) facing in, the outside edge when turned.
  + Press the rubber flat on the wall where no foothold exists (#tref("smearing", body: [smearing], show-page: false)).
  + Pull each hold away from the way it faces.
  + Grip each hold only as hard as it needs.
]

==== What problems look like

Walls are mostly vertical or slightly overhanging. Jugs give way to #jt("incut") edges, whose lip curls back towards you. You also meet your first holds to pull sideways. Footholds are smaller and sometimes off to the side, so more moves depend on a weight shift before the reach. Moves are still #jt("static"): you could stop at any point. Problems have five to eight moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Use #tref("edging", body: [edging]) on the outside of the shoe as well as the inside. Use #tref("smearing", body: [smearing]) where there is no foothold. Pull a #tref("sidepull", body: [sidepull]) towards you. A #tref("high-step", body: [high step]) needs a #tref("rockover", body: [rockover]) to finish it: move your hips across the high foot, then stand up. Many V1s ask you to bring both hands onto one hold (#tref("matching", inner: true)). Others ask you to turn both knees out so your hips come in (#tref("frog", inner: true)). In a corner or between two #tref("volume", body: [volumes]) (big bolted-on shapes), push your feet out against both sides (#tref("stemming", inner: true)).

#grade-techniques(1)

==== Focus

- Before each reach, shift your hips sideways over the foot you will push from. Then reach.
- Use the inside edge of the shoe when you face the wall. Switch to the outside edge when your hip turns in.
- Grip no harder than you need. On easy problems, relax your fingers until they start to slip. Then add a little.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Read which way each handhold faces. A hold that faces left puts your body to its right (Chapter 1).
- Look for footholds that bring your hips under the next handhold.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific.

==== Why people stall here

- *Square-on climbing.* You keep your chest to the wall and stand on both toe tips for every move. Sideways reaches then fall short. Fix: square on suits most vertical walls, so on easy problems that lean towards you, practise turning one hip into the wall (#tref("hip-turn", inner: true)).
- *Over-gripping.* Fix: the loose-grip habit above.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V2

== V2 (Font 5+) <grade-2>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V2][
  + Turn one hip into the wall on steep ground (#tref("hip-turn", body: [hip turn], show-page: false)).
  + Put out a #tref("flag", body: [flag], show-page: false) before a hand lets go.
  + Lean off two sideways holds that face the same way (#tref("layback", body: [layback], show-page: false)).
  + Bring your feet high under an #tref("undercling", body: [undercling], show-page: false) before you reach.
  + On the steep, hang on straight arms so your biceps can rest.
]

==== What problems look like

Gentle overhangs of about 10–25° become common. Holds include ones you pull sideways or pull up from below. Some moves only work with your body turned sideways to the wall. Footholds no longer sit under every handhold, so you often reach from one foot while the other has nothing useful. Problems have five to ten moves. A short #jt("dynamic") move (one that uses momentum, so you can't stop halfway) may appear, but most V2s still go statically.

==== Techniques that start to matter

On the steep, learn to turn a hip in (#tref("hip-turn", inner: true)); the #tref("backstep", body: [backstep]) builds on this from V3. The #tref("flag", body: [side flag]) stops the #tref("barn-door", body: [barn door]) swing when one hand lets go. Two sideways holds facing the same way ask for a #tref("layback", body: [layback]). An #tref("undercling", body: [undercling]) at chest height asks you to bring your feet high. Some moves, especially on #tref("traverse", body: [traverses]), ask you to swap feet on one hold (#tref("foot-swap", inner: true)). On #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]) (small edges held on the fingertips), make the #tref("half-crimp", body: [half crimp]) your default from the start, so you don't fall into the habit of full crimping. As footholds shrink, #tref("slab") starts to matter. Problems that finish on top ask for a #tref("mantle", body: [mantle]) and a #tref("top-out", body: [top-out]).

A forum poster says some V2s can still be pulled on muscle #src("F44"). Avoid building that habit. On big holds low on the wall, start practising a partial #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knee]), small #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoints]) and the #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]) (this book's suggestion).

#grade-techniques(2)

==== Focus

- Learn to tell front-on moves from side-on moves. Switch between them on purpose.
- On an overhang, put the outside edge of your shoe on the foothold and turn that hip into the wall. The shoulder on that side rises, which lengthens your reach.
- When one hand is about to let go, press the opposite foot into the wall as a flag. Do it *before* you move.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Two holds facing the same way, such as two sideways holds facing left, mean a layback, or from V3 a backstep.
- A single foothold far out to one side, with nothing on the other side, means a flag, or from V3 a backstep.

==== Physical side

Nothing specific.

==== Why people stall here

- *Barn-dooring.* You swing off when one hand releases. Fix: the flag above, *before* the hand moves.
- *Arms tire on the steep.* Fix: straighten your arms and turn a hip in, so your biceps can rest.

#ix("Plateau!at V2") V2 is a known plateau: one gym runs a movement clinic for climbers stuck there #src("F36"). The way out is probably learning to climb side-on (judgement).

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V3

== V3 (Font 6A/6A+) <grade-3>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V3][
  + Catch poor holds at the top of your rise (#tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint], show-page: false)).
  + Hook a heel on hip-high holds to pull your hips in (#tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook], show-page: false)).
  + Drive your toes into the wall to keep your feet on overhangs.
  + Sink your hips, then drive with your legs to create momentum.
  + Spend one session in four on the style you avoid, slab or steep.
]

==== What problems look like

V3s come in two families. *Steep* problems sit on 20–40° overhangs with mid-sized edges and rounded holds. Your feet stay on only if you pull with them. *Slab and vertical* problems have blank wall, #tref("volume", body: [volumes]) and small edges for the feet. There, balance is the #jt("crux") (the hardest move). Many V3s are built around one clear move, such as a long span or a hook with the heel. Problems have four to ten moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

Learn the #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoint]): catch the hold at the top of a small upward movement, when you have stopped rising and feel lighter. Use your first #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook]) on a good hold to pull your hips in. Learn #tref("overhang", body: [overhang]) basics: #tref("toe-pull") and #tref("body-tension") keep your feet on, while a #tref("backstep", body: [backstep]) or a partial #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knee]) turns a hip to the wall. Where you can't turn in, a #tref("lock-off", body: [lock-off]) holds you for a slow reach. Your hands meet the #tref("sloper", body: [sloper]), the #tref("pinch", body: [pinch]) and the #tref("gaston", body: [gaston]), a hold you push outwards with your elbow out. On big holds, start practising the #tref("lunge", body: [one-handed dyno]), the #tref("double-dyno", body: [double dyno]) and the #tref("run-and-jump", body: [run-and-jump]) (this book's suggestion).

#grade-techniques(3)

==== Focus

- *Toes into the wall on steep ground.* Push through the front of the shoe into the hold, not down along the wall #src("F41").
- *Create momentum.* Sink your hips a little, then drive up with your legs. Your arms guide the movement rather than haul it #src("F33", "F39").
- *Flash more.* Read each V3 fully before you touch it. Then try to #jt("flash") it: climb it first go, without falling.

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Can you catch the crux hold while moving, or must you arrive static (#tref("lock-off", inner: true))? You can catch a deep hold with a lip. A poor sloper needs a deadpoint or a slow reach.
- Is there a foothold at hip height on the side you move towards? It may be meant for a heel hook.
- On slab, find where you can stand. Then find blank wall clean enough to #tref("smearing", body: [smear]) on.
- On a pinch, find the thumb side before you set off.

// these two go over the page together, not the last alone
#keep-together[
==== Physical side

Lattice tests a #jt("max hang")#ix("Max hang!averages by grade"): the most extra weight you can hang for a few seconds. In its lowest band, V1–V4, climbers add about 19~kg on two arms #src("F1"). That figure mixes men and women over four grades, so many V3 climbers climb well on less (judgement).

==== Why people stall here

- *Feet cutting on the steep.* Fix: keep both feet pressing into the wall, even the one you are about to move. Keep your hips close #src("F41").
- *Climbing one style.*#ix("Plateau!climbing one style") You climb all the steep V3s and avoid the slabs, or the reverse. Lattice's coaches see plateaus#ix("Plateau!when it happens") "somewhere between V3–V6", usually in the first couple of years. They list avoiding weak styles as a main cause #src("F9"). Fix: spend one session in four on the style you avoid.
]

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V4

== V4 (Font 6B/6B+) <grade-4>

#remember[Five things to keep in mind at V4][
  + Start big steep moves from straight arms, hips back and down.
  + Pull with your #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook], show-page: false): bend the knee and drag your hips in.
  + Use a #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook], show-page: false) to stop the swing when a hand lets go.
  + Keep both toes pressing in when your hands reach far (#tref("body-tension", body: [body tension], show-page: false)).
  + Plan your body position for the hardest move before you start.
]

==== What problems look like

Expect overhangs of 30–45°, or vertical walls with poor feet. #jt("crimp", body: [Crimps]) and rounded holds replace most jugs. Footholds are small edges or the faces of #tref("volume", body: [volumes]). A V4 often combines two or three techniques, e.g. a heel hook that sets up a long reach. One or two moves need momentum, e.g. a jump to a big hold or a #jt("coordination move") (a timed sequence of jumps or swings). Problems have four to eight hard moves.

==== Techniques that start to matter

The #tref("heel-hook", body: [heel hook]) becomes an active pull: bend the knee and pull with the #jt("hamstring") (the muscle at the back of the thigh) to draw your hips in. Add the #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook]) to stop a swing and to keep your feet on a #tref("roof", body: [roof]). Squeezing between two holds becomes a technique of its own (#tref("compression", inner: true)). Gyms start to set the #tref("lunge", body: [one-handed dyno]) and the #tref("dyno", body: [dyno]) here, so you need #tref("latching", body: [latching]) to hold the catch and #tref("cutting-loose", body: [cutting loose]) to control the swing. The #tref("run-and-jump", body: [run-and-jump]) appears on problems set in a competition style. Your hands meet the #tref("pocket", body: [pocket]). On rounded holds the thumb starts to help (#tref("thumb-catch", inner: true)). By now, momentum is a normal tool, not a last resort.

#grade-techniques(4)

==== Focus

- *Choose #jt("static") or #jt("dynamic").* For big moves on overhangs, start with straight arms. Sit your hips back and down, then drive from your feet. Newer climbers tend to #tref("lock-off", body: [lock off]) too much #src("F39").
- *Body tension.* When your hands are far above you or out to the side, keep pushing through both toes. If your feet go light, pull on the footholds with your toes.
- *Repeat, then reach up.* Repeat V4s until they feel smooth. Put real attempts into at least one V5 every session. Lattice counts learning chances, not #jt("send", body: [sends]) (clean climbs of a problem) #src("F9").

==== Reading problems at this grade

- Find the #jt("crux") first, then work backwards. What body position must you be in to start the crux move?
- Find the move where your feet will swing off. Decide how you will catch the swing before you leave the ground.

#lead-label[Look for]
- heel-hook holds: a horizontal edge or volume at hip height, out to the side.
- toe-hook features: a hold or volume edge that faces down or sideways at foot level.

==== Physical side

#ix("Finger strength!by grade") Lattice's V1–V4 band adds about 19~kg on a two-arm #jt("max hang") #src("F1").

==== Why people stall here

- *Muscling through.* You can pull hard, so you never learn the position that makes the move easy.
- *Never trying V5s.* You repeat what you can already climb.

The next section covers both.

// ------------------------------------------------------------------ V4 to V5

== From V4 to V5 <grades-v4-v5>

#remember[Five things that get you from V4 to V5][
  + Assume technique, not strength, stops you until a symptom says otherwise (judgement, from the coaches' advice in "Technique or strength?").
  + Work three to five V5s, in different styles, over several sessions.
  + After each fall, check your hips, your #jt("off foot") and your arms.
  + Start steep moves on straight arms and drive from your feet; #tref("lock-off", body: [lock off], show-page: false) less.
  + Drill partial #tref("drop-knee", body: [drop knees], show-page: false), toe hooks and #tref("compression", body: [compression], show-page: false) on V3s and V4s first.
]

#ix("Plateau!V4 to V5", main: true)
This section is for you if you climb most V4s but haven't #jt("send", body: [sent]) a V5. The V5 plateau is common enough that gyms name clinics after it #src("F33", "F35").

==== What changes between V4 and V5

#lead-in[In the table, #jt("beta") is the sequence of moves that works. Most rows come from gym clinics and forum reports, not measurement. The forum reports are about the step to V6, which shows where V5 is heading.]

#gtable(
  columns: (28mm, 1fr, 1fr),
  table.header(th[], th[Typical V4], th[Typical V5]),
  [*Holds*], [Edges and rounded holds with some lip; a jug to rest on], [Smaller #jt("crimp", body: [crimps]), rounder holds, pinches; few rests #src("F48", "F46")],
  [*Wall angle*], [30–45°, or vertical with poor feet], [More problems on the steepest walls, 40–50° (judgement)],
  [*Footholds*], [Small edges, volumes], [Smaller edges or none; tension holds the feet on #src("F48")\; feet often swing off],
  [*The #jt("crux")*], [Several solutions work], [Usually one technique done precisely, such as a #tref("toe-hook", body: [toe hook]) to stop a swing (judgement)],
  [*Dynamic moves*], [Deadpoints, lunges, the odd dyno or coordination move (judgement)], [Committed #tref("deadpoint", body: [deadpoints]), #tref("dyno", body: [dynos]) and coordination moves such as #tref("run-and-jump", body: [run-and-jump]) #src("F34")],
  [*Body tension*], [Needed on steep moves (judgement)], [Needed to keep the feet on through hand moves #src("F35", "F48")],
